WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:05.001
Heart failure is not a single signal. Physiological changes

00:00:05.001 --> 00:00:09.452
appear through movements, rhythms and measurements interpreted in context.

00:00:12.717 --> 00:00:18.308
Non-invasive and minimally invasive sensors can produce information about

00:00:18.308 --> 00:00:23.898
cardiac and circulatory physiology without requiring identical clinical procedures.

00:00:27.717 --> 00:00:32.442
The chapter surveys sensor technologies intended to help

00:00:32.442 --> 00:00:37.167
identify or predict heart-failure trajectories, with different technological trade-offs.

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:45.843
Motion, noise, timing and patient variability all influence interpretation.

00:00:45.843 --> 00:00:51.135
A useful measurement is not automatically a useful clinical decision.

00:00:54.758 --> 00:01:00.465
Predictive performance needs evidence from realistic populations and

00:01:00.465 --> 00:01:06.172
robust external testing; practical accessibility matters alongside technical sophistication.

00:01:10.008 --> 00:01:15.430
This 2022 book chapter maps the promise and constraints of

00:01:15.430 --> 00:01:20.852
measuring physiological warning signs. The chapter supplies the cited technologies.
